  Worst case Restore Ability can be blanked to water.  Those who like to
polymorph can also polymorph it.  These are also true for a spellbook of
restore ability.  The spell of restore ability can also be used when
meleeing a mind flayer, when a unihorn is too unreliable.  (No, I don't
think it's a good idea.  Some  players do it anyway.)  Restore ability
is also the perfect spell to leave expired as a means of confusion in
the End Game.  So there's something good and almost necessary that being
a "useless" spell is the perfect qualification for.

  Back to polymorphing the spellbook of restore ability.  The crowning
gift of one should be blessed.  That means it can be polymorphed more
before disappearing.  It also means it can always be read successfully,
no matter the spell difficulty.  (Be careful not to read it if it's a
known spell and it's been polyd/read 3+ times.)  Getting a lot of known
spells this way is good for a Monk, since they can get to at least basic
in all spell schools.  (Wizards are the only other class unrestricted in
all spell schools.)  Toss in the Monk's starting robe and spell casting
becomes very possible.  Additionally, once the spells are known writing
the spellbook is 100% guaranteed.

  Almost everything has some use, even if it's not the primary purpose.
A big part of getting good at NetHack is learning all those uses.  It's
all part of "There's more than one way to do it."  (Which is not
powergaming by any stretch of the imagination.)

  Yes, it takes more work to make use of it.  Welcome to NetHack.


